{{smaller block|''Tunick'' or Tunicat, a Jerkin, Jacket, or sleeveless coat, formerly worn by Princes. {{right|''Blount'', Glossographia (1670).}}}}
'''2.''' At the present time, a garment generally loose, but gathered or girded at the waist, worn by women, usually an outer garment; a sort of wrap or coat for street wear.
{{smaller block|Her Majesty wore a white satin petticoat, over which was a silver llama ''tunic'', trimmed with silver and white blonde lace. {{right|''First Year of a Silken Reign'', p. 230.}}}}
'''3.''' ''Eccles.'', a vestment worn over the alb in the Roman Catholic Church and in some Anglican churches by the subdeacon or epistler at the celebration of the mass or holy communion. {{smaller|It is similar in shape and color to the dalmatic, but sometimes smaller and with less ornamentation. The bishop’s tunic is worn under the dalmatic, and is shorter than the subdeacon’s. See ''tunicle''.}}
'''4.''' A military surcoat.—'''5.''' In the British army, the ordinary fatigue-coat: applied usually to the coat of a private, but sometimes to that of an officer. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|“Please show me your Victoria Cross.” “It’s on my ''tunic'', and that ’s in my quarters in camp.” {{right|''J. H. Ewing'', Story of a Short Life, vii.}}}}

